          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"In apocalyptic contexts, beasts primarily symbolize brutal, anti-God kingdoms, empires, rulers, or political systems, with disputed historical or eschatological identifications. Some responses also recognize animal distinctions as symbols for Gentiles or kinds of people.\",\"book_summary\":\"A beast symbolizes a nation or kingdom under earthly rule, especially in its wild or predatory aspect; distinct beasts represent distinct kingdoms, while clean and unclean beasts distinguish kinds of people.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"A beast is a hostile or oppressive earthly political kingdom, empire, or ruler.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"A beast is an earthly nation or kingdom, with animal categories also capable of representing categories of people.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_BROADENS\",\"extension_relation\":\"BOOK_SUPERSET\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book retains political kingdoms as the central referent but does not require them to be explicitly anti-God, persecuting, or eschatological. It therefore extends the symbol to earthly nations or kingdoms more generally and adds clean-versus-unclean beasts as classifications of people. This latter use was recognized as a minority alternative in the consensus.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no contradiction at the core: both identify beasts with earthly kingdoms or political powers. The material difference is that the consensus foregrounds oppressive, anti-God power, whereas the book makes wild or predatory character frequent rather than defining and broadens the term to human categories.\",\"rationale\":\"The book preserves the consensus’s central kingdom/political-power identification, so it does not replace the referent. Its extension is materially broader, however: ordinary earthly nations can qualify without the consensus’s usual hostile or persecuting condition, and clean/unclean animals can signify kinds of people. That is a boundary refinement rather than a divergent identification.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Daniel 7:17 and 8:20–21 directly support beasts representing kings or kingdoms, and Acts 10:12,28 supports animal purity distinctions being applied to people. However, these citations alone do not establish that every nation under earthly rule is a beast, nor do they directly formulate the book’s general claim about wild or predatory national character.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
